Now let me add the DMA function prototypes and implement the functions one by one. Let me start with the most fundamental function: `esp32p4_ciu_sendcmd` which sends a command to the CIU.

Looking at the ESP32-S3 implementation:
```c
static int esp32s3_ciu_sendcmd(uint32_t cmd, uint32_t arg)
{
  clock_t watchtime;

  watchtime = clock_systime_ticks();

  while ((esp32s3_getreg(ESP32S3_SDMMC_CMD) & SDMMC_CMD_STARTCMD) != 0)
    {
      if (watchtime - clock_systime_ticks() > SDCARD_CMDTIMEOUT)
        {
          mcerr("TMO Timed out (%08X)\n",
                esp32s3_getreg(ESP32S3_SDMMC_CMD));
          return 1;
        }
    }

  /* Set command arg reg */

  cmd |= SDMMC_CMD_STARTCMD | SDMMC_CMD_USE_HOLE;

  esp32s3_putreg(arg, ESP32S3_SDMMC_CMDARG);
  esp32s3_putreg(cmd, ESP32S3_SDMMC_CMD);

  mcinfo("cmd=0x%x arg=0x%x\n", cmd, arg);

  return 0;
}
```

For ESP32P4, I should use the LL library functions instead of direct register access. The LL library provides:
- `sdmmc_ll_is_command_taken(hw)` - checks if command is taken by CIU
- `sdmmc_ll_set_command_arg(hw, arg)` - sets command argument


- `sdmmc_ll_set_command(hw, cmd)` - sets command

The command structure for ESP32P4 is `sdmmc_hw_cmd_t` (which is `sdhost_cmd_reg_t`), and I need to convert NuttX command encoding to the hardware register format. I'll start implementing `esp32p4_ciu_sendcmd` using the LL library functions.

